    </div>
    <div class="sect2">
+    <h3 id="pveceph_cephx_how_it_works">
+     <span>
+      8.11. Cephx Authentication
+     </span>
+     <a class="headerlink" href="#pveceph_cephx_how_it_works" title="Permalink to this heading">
+     </a>
+    </h3>
+    <div class="paragraph">
+     <p>
+      Cephx
+      <span class="footnote">
+       <br/>
+       [Cephx authorization and authentication protocol
+       <a href="https://docs.ceph.com/en/tentacle/dev/cephx_protocol/">
+        https://docs.ceph.com/en/tentacle/dev/cephx_protocol/
+       </a>
+       ]
+       <br/>
+      </span>
+      protects access to a Ceph cluster.
+     </p>
+    </div>
+    <div class="paragraph">
+     <p>
+      Every Ceph service and client, including storage consumers and tools,
+authenticates to a monitor as a Ceph user, such as
+      <span class="monospaced">
+       client.admin
+      </span>
+      or
+      <span class="monospaced">
+       osd.3
+      </span>
+      ,
+with that user’s long-term secret key. The monitor then issues temporary
+credentials called tickets:
+     </p>
+    </div>
+    <div style="overflow-x:auto">
+     <table class="tableblock frame-all grid-all" style="
+width:100%;
+">
+      <col style="width:28%;"/>
+      <col style="width:71%;"/>
+      <thead>
+       <tr>
+        <th class="tableblock halign-left valign-top">
+         Term
+        </th>
+        <th class="tableblock halign-left valign-top">
+         Purpose
+        </th>
+       </tr>
+      </thead>
+      <tbody>
+       <tr>
+        <td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top">
+         <p class="tableblock">
+          User key
+         </p>
+        </td>
+        <td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top">
+         <p class="tableblock">
+          Long-term secret used by a client or Ceph service to authenticate to a monitor.
+         </p>
+        </td>
+       </tr>
+       <tr>
+        <td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top">
+         <p class="tableblock">
+          Monitor ticket
+         </p>
+        </td>
+        <td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top">
+         <p class="tableblock">
+          Lets an authenticated Ceph user request tickets for Ceph services.
+         </p>
+        </td>
+       </tr>
+       <tr>
+        <td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top">
+         <p class="tableblock">
+          Service ticket
+         </p>
+        </td>
+        <td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top">
+         <p class="tableblock">
+          Grants temporary access to an OSD, manager, or metadata server.
+         </p>
+        </td>
+       </tr>
+       <tr>
+        <td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top">
+         <p class="tableblock">
+          Rotating service key
+         </p>
+        </td>
+        <td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top">
+         <p class="tableblock">
+          Lets Ceph services validate service tickets. Monitors
+replace these keys automatically.
+         </p>
+        </td>
+       </tr>
+      </tbody>
+     </table>
+    </div>
+    <div class="paragraph">
+     <p>
+      A running client usually reads its user key only when it starts. Updating a
+saved key or keyring file does not update that process. Existing tickets and
+connections can also remain usable for some time. A key rotation must
+therefore account for running and temporarily disconnected clients.
+     </p>
+    </div>
+    <div class="sect3">
+     <h4 id="pveceph_keyrings">
+      8.11.1. Keyring Locations
+      <a class="headerlink" href="#pveceph_keyrings" title="Permalink to this heading">
+      </a>
+     </h4>
+     <div class="paragraph">
+      <p>
+       The authoritative
+       <span class="monospaced">
+        client.admin
+       </span>
+       keyring in a Proxmox VE Ceph cluster is
+       <span class="monospaced">
+        /etc/pve/priv/ceph.client.admin.keyring
+       </span>
+       , not the copy in
+       <span class="monospaced">
+        /etc/ceph
+       </span>
+       . The
+       <a href="#chapter_pmxcfs">
+        Proxmox cluster file system (pmxcfs)
+       </a>
+       synchronizes it to
+every node. Credentials for managed Ceph storages are below
+       <span class="monospaced">
+        /etc/pve/priv/ceph/
+       </span>
+       .
+      </p>
+     </div>
+     <div class="paragraph">
+      <p>
+       Keyrings below
+       <span class="monospaced">
+        /var/lib/ceph
+       </span>
+       belong to one Ceph service and are local to its
+node.
+       <span class="monospaced">
+        /etc/pve/priv/ceph.mon.keyring
+       </span>
+       contains both the shared
+       <span class="monospaced">
+        mon.
+       </span>
+       key and
+       <span class="monospaced">
+        client.admin
+       </span>
+       . Always merge updates into this file instead of overwriting it.
+       <span class="monospaced">
+        pveceph mon create
+       </span>
+       uses its
+       <span class="monospaced">
+        mon.
+       </span>
+       entry when it initializes a monitor.
+      </p>
+     </div>
+    </div>
+    <div class="sect3">
+     <h4 id="pveceph_cephx_lockbox">
+      8.11.2. Encrypted OSD Lockbox Keys
+      <a class="headerlink" href="#pveceph_cephx_lockbox" title="Permalink to this heading">
+      </a>
+     </h4>
+     <div class="paragraph">
+      <p>
+       Each encrypted OSD uses a dedicated Ceph user named
+       <span class="monospaced">
+        client.osd-lockbox.&lt;UUID&gt;
+       </span>
+       . Its authentication key is called the lockbox key.
+It is not the disk-encryption key itself: it lets the OSD retrieve that key
+from Ceph and unlock its storage when it starts. Ceph and the OSD’s block
+device must contain matching copies of the lockbox key.
+      </p>
+     </div>
+     <div class="admonitionblock">
+      <table>
+       <tr>
+        <td class="icon">
